1
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:08,000
It fell out as Wemmick had told me it would, that I had an early opportunity of comparing

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,840
my guardian’s establishment with that of his cashier and clerk. My guardian was in his room,

3
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:19,240
washing his hands with his scented soap, when I went into the office from Walworth;

4
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:22,719
and he called me to him, and gave me the invitation for myself and friends

5
00:00:22,719 --> 00:00:28,919
which Wemmick had prepared me to receive. “No ceremony,” he stipulated, “and no dinner dress,

6
00:00:28,919 --> 00:00:35,080
and say to-morrow.” I asked him where we should come to (for I had no idea where he lived),

7
00:00:35,080 --> 00:00:40,477
and I believe it was in his general objection to make anything like an admission, that he replied,

8
00:00:40,478 --> 00:00:46,398
“Come here, and I’ll take you home with me.” I embrace this opportunity of remarking that he

9
00:00:46,398 --> 00:00:53,477
washed his clients off, as if he were a surgeon or a dentist. He had a closet in his room,

10
00:00:53,478 --> 00:00:59,559
fitted up for the purpose, which smelt of the scented soap like a perfumer’s shop. It

11
00:00:59,559 --> 00:01:04,359
had an unusually large jack-towel on a roller inside the door, and he would wash his hands,

12
00:01:04,359 --> 00:01:09,477
and wipe them and dry them all over this towel, whenever he came in from a police court or

13
00:01:09,478 --> 00:01:15,520
dismissed a client from his room. When I and my friends repaired to him at six o’clock next day,

14
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:20,359
he seemed to have been engaged on a case of a darker complexion than usual, for we found

15
00:01:20,359 --> 00:01:25,958
him with his head butted into this closet, not only washing his hands, but laving his face and

16
00:01:25,958 --> 00:01:32,278
gargling his throat. And even when he had done all that, and had gone all round the jack-towel,

17
00:01:32,278 --> 00:01:38,199
he took out his penknife and scraped the case out of his nails before he put his coat on.

18
00:01:38,199 --> 00:01:42,240
There were some people slinking about as usual when we passed out into the street,

19
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:46,240
who were evidently anxious to speak with him; but there was something so

20
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:50,359
conclusive in the halo of scented soap which encircled his presence,

21
00:01:50,359 --> 00:01:57,000
that they gave it up for that day. As we walked along westward, he was recognised ever and again

22
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,199
by some face in the crowd of the streets, and whenever that happened he talked louder to me;

23
00:02:03,199 --> 00:02:09,477
but he never otherwise recognised anybody, or took notice that anybody recognised him.

24
00:02:09,478 --> 00:02:14,919
He conducted us to Gerrard Street, Soho, to a house on the south side of that street. Rather

25
00:02:14,919 --> 00:02:21,638
a stately house of its kind, but dolefully in want of painting, and with dirty windows.

26
00:02:21,638 --> 00:02:27,599
He took out his key and opened the door, and we all went into a stone hall, bare, gloomy,

27
00:02:27,599 --> 00:02:33,958
and little used. So, up a dark brown staircase into a series of three dark brown rooms on the

28
00:02:33,958 --> 00:02:39,520
first floor. There were carved garlands on the panelled walls, and as he stood

29
00:02:39,520 --> 00:02:44,958
among them giving us welcome, I know what kind of loops I thought they looked like.

30
00:02:44,958 --> 00:02:49,398
Dinner was laid in the best of these rooms; the second was his dressing-room;

31
00:02:49,398 --> 00:02:54,159
the third, his bedroom. He told us that he held the whole house,

32
00:02:54,159 --> 00:03:00,438
but rarely used more of it than we saw. The table was comfortably laid—no silver in the service,

33
00:03:00,438 --> 00:03:06,080
of course—and at the side of his chair was a capacious dumb-waiter, with a variety of

34
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:12,317
bottles and decanters on it, and four dishes of fruit for dessert. I noticed throughout,

35
00:03:12,317 --> 00:03:18,080
that he kept everything under his own hand, and distributed everything himself.

36
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:22,039
There was a bookcase in the room; I saw from the backs of the books,

37
00:03:22,039 --> 00:03:29,240
that they were about evidence, criminal law, criminal biography, trials, acts of Parliament,

38
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:35,519
and such things. The furniture was all very solid and good, like his watch-chain. It had

39
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:40,680
an official look, however, and there was nothing merely ornamental to be seen. In

40
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:46,039
a corner was a little table of papers with a shaded lamp: so that he seemed to bring the

41
00:03:46,039 --> 00:03:52,559
office home with him in that respect too, and to wheel it out of an evening and fall to work.

42
00:03:52,559 --> 00:03:58,638
As he had scarcely seen my three companions until now,—for he and I had walked together,—he stood

43
00:03:58,638 --> 00:04:05,278
on the hearth-rug, after ringing the bell, and took a searching look at them. To my surprise,

44
00:04:05,278 --> 00:04:10,240
he seemed at once to be principally if not solely interested in Drummle.

45
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:15,317
“Pip,” said he, putting his large hand on my shoulder and moving me to the window,

46
00:04:15,317 --> 00:04:19,558
“I don’t know one from the other. Who’s the Spider?”

47
00:04:19,559 --> 00:04:21,478
“The spider?” said I.

48
00:04:21,478 --> 00:04:24,680
“The blotchy, sprawly, sulky fellow.”

49
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:29,478
“That’s Bentley Drummle,” I replied; “the one with the delicate face is Startop.”

50
00:04:30,638 --> 00:04:35,520
Not making the least account of “the one with the delicate face,” he returned,

51
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:39,879
“Bentley Drummle is his name, is it? I like the look of that fellow.”

52
00:04:39,879 --> 00:04:45,240
He immediately began to talk to Drummle: not at all deterred by his replying in his heavy

53
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:51,080
reticent way, but apparently led on by it to screw discourse out of him. I was looking at

54
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:57,759
the two, when there came between me and them the housekeeper, with the first dish for the table.

55
00:04:57,759 --> 00:05:04,478
She was a woman of about forty, I supposed,—but I may have thought her younger than she was. Rather

56
00:05:04,478 --> 00:05:11,759
tall, of a lithe nimble figure, extremely pale, with large faded eyes, and a quantity of streaming

57
00:05:11,759 --> 00:05:17,359
hair. I cannot say whether any diseased affection of the heart caused her lips to be parted as

58
00:05:17,359 --> 00:05:23,438
if she were panting, and her face to bear a curious expression of suddenness and flutter;

59
00:05:23,438 --> 00:05:27,679
but I know that I had been to see Macbeth at the theatre, a night or two before,

60
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:32,680
and that her face looked to me as if it were all disturbed by fiery air, like the faces

61
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:39,638
I had seen rise out of the Witches’ caldron. She set the dish on, touched my guardian quietly

62
00:05:39,638 --> 00:05:46,039
on the arm with a finger to notify that dinner was ready, and vanished. We took our seats at

63
00:05:46,039 --> 00:05:52,798
the round table, and my guardian kept Drummle on one side of him, while Startop sat on the other.

64
00:05:52,798 --> 00:05:57,958
It was a noble dish of fish that the housekeeper had put on table, and we had a joint of equally

65
00:05:57,958 --> 00:06:05,159
choice mutton afterwards, and then an equally choice bird. Sauces, wines, all the accessories

66
00:06:05,159 --> 00:06:11,638
we wanted, and all of the best, were given out by our host from his dumb-waiter; and when they had

67
00:06:11,638 --> 00:06:18,080
made the circuit of the table, he always put them back again. Similarly, he dealt us clean

68
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:24,199
plates and knives and forks, for each course, and dropped those just disused into two baskets on

69
00:06:24,199 --> 00:06:31,519
the ground by his chair. No other attendant than the housekeeper appeared. She set on every dish;

70
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:37,798
and I always saw in her face, a face rising out of the caldron. Years afterwards, I made

71
00:06:37,798 --> 00:06:42,879
a dreadful likeness of that woman, by causing a face that had no other natural resemblance

72
00:06:42,879 --> 00:06:50,680
to it than it derived from flowing hair to pass behind a bowl of flaming spirits in a dark room.

73
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:55,080
Induced to take particular notice of the housekeeper, both by her own striking

74
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:59,519
appearance and by Wemmick’s preparation, I observed that whenever she was in the room

75
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:04,359
she kept her eyes attentively on my guardian, and that she would remove her hands from any

76
00:07:04,359 --> 00:07:10,359
dish she put before him, hesitatingly, as if she dreaded his calling her back,

77
00:07:10,359 --> 00:07:16,519
and wanted him to speak when she was nigh, if he had anything to say. I fancied that I could

78
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:23,359
detect in his manner a consciousness of this, and a purpose of always holding her in suspense.

79
00:07:23,359 --> 00:07:29,838
Dinner went off gayly, and although my guardian seemed to follow rather than originate subjects,

80
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:35,240
I knew that he wrenched the weakest part of our dispositions out of us. For myself,

81
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:41,679
I found that I was expressing my tendency to lavish expenditure, and to patronise Herbert,

82
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:48,159
and to boast of my great prospects, before I quite knew that I had opened my lips. It was so with

83
00:07:48,159 --> 00:07:54,240
all of us, but with no one more than Drummle: the development of whose inclination to gird

84
00:07:54,240 --> 00:08:01,199
in a grudging and suspicious way at the rest, was screwed out of him before the fish was taken off.

85
00:08:01,199 --> 00:08:05,879
It was not then, but when we had got to the cheese, that our conversation turned

86
00:08:05,879 --> 00:08:11,240
upon our rowing feats, and that Drummle was rallied for coming up behind of a night in

87
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:17,558
that slow amphibious way of his. Drummle upon this, informed our host that he much preferred

88
00:08:17,559 --> 00:08:23,759
our room to our company, and that as to skill he was more than our master, and that as to

89
00:08:23,759 --> 00:08:30,520
strength he could scatter us like chaff. By some invisible agency, my guardian wound him up to a

90
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:36,240
pitch little short of ferocity about this trifle; and he fell to baring and spanning his arm to show

91
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:42,637
how muscular it was, and we all fell to baring and spanning our arms in a ridiculous manner.

92
00:08:42,638 --> 00:08:48,759
Now the housekeeper was at that time clearing the table; my guardian, taking no heed of her,

93
00:08:48,759 --> 00:08:53,879
but with the side of his face turned from her, was leaning back in his chair biting the side of his

94
00:08:53,879 --> 00:09:01,000
forefinger and showing an interest in Drummle, that, to me, was quite inexplicable. Suddenly,

95
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,398
he clapped his large hand on the housekeeper’s, like a trap, as she stretched it across the

96
00:09:05,398 --> 00:09:12,797
table. So suddenly and smartly did he do this, that we all stopped in our foolish contention.

97
00:09:12,798 --> 00:09:20,240
“If you talk of strength,” said Mr. Jaggers, “I’ll show you a wrist. Molly, let them see your wrist.”

98
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:22,679
Her entrapped hand was on the table,

99
00:09:22,680 --> 00:09:28,599
but she had already put her other hand behind her waist. “Master,” she said,

100
00:09:28,599 --> 00:09:35,159
in a low voice, with her eyes attentively and entreatingly fixed upon him. “Don’t.”

101
00:09:35,159 --> 00:09:38,278
“I’ll show you a wrist,” repeated Mr. Jaggers,

102
00:09:38,278 --> 00:09:43,519
with an immovable determination to show it. “Molly, let them see your wrist.”

103
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:47,159
“Master,” she again murmured. “Please!”

104
00:09:47,159 --> 00:09:52,278
“Molly,” said Mr. Jaggers, not looking at her, but obstinately looking at the

105
00:09:52,278 --> 00:09:59,000
opposite side of the room, “let them see both your wrists. Show them. Come!”

106
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:03,840
He took his hand from hers, and turned that wrist up on the table. She brought

107
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:09,159
her other hand from behind her, and held the two out side by side. The

108
00:10:09,159 --> 00:10:14,840
last wrist was much disfigured,—deeply scarred and scarred across and across.

109
00:10:15,398 --> 00:10:19,679
When she held her hands out she took her eyes from Mr. Jaggers, and turned them watchfully on

110
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:25,317
every one of the rest of us in succession. “There’s power here,” said Mr. Jaggers,

111
00:10:25,317 --> 00:10:31,398
coolly tracing out the sinews with his forefinger. “Very few men have the power of wrist that this

112
00:10:31,398 --> 00:10:37,558
woman has. It’s remarkable what mere force of grip there is in these hands. I have had occasion

113
00:10:37,559 --> 00:10:44,840
to notice many hands; but I never saw stronger in that respect, man’s or woman’s, than these.”

114
00:10:44,840 --> 00:10:50,080
While he said these words in a leisurely, critical style, she continued to look at every one of us

115
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:57,317
in regular succession as we sat. The moment he ceased, she looked at him again. “That’ll do,

116
00:10:57,317 --> 00:11:02,558
Molly,” said Mr. Jaggers, giving her a slight nod; “you have been admired,

117
00:11:02,559 --> 00:11:08,599
and can go.” She withdrew her hands and went out of the room, and Mr. Jaggers,

118
00:11:08,599 --> 00:11:14,599
putting the decanters on from his dumb-waiter, filled his glass and passed round the wine.

119
00:11:14,599 --> 00:11:18,879
“At half-past nine, gentlemen,” said he, “we must break up. Pray make the

120
00:11:18,879 --> 00:11:26,039
best use of your time. I am glad to see you all. Mr. Drummle, I drink to you.”

121
00:11:26,039 --> 00:11:30,477
If his object in singling out Drummle were to bring him out still more, it perfectly

122
00:11:30,478 --> 00:11:36,520
succeeded. In a sulky triumph, Drummle showed his morose depreciation of the rest of us,

123
00:11:36,520 --> 00:11:41,520
in a more and more offensive degree, until he became downright intolerable.

124
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:44,958
Through all his stages, Mr. Jaggers followed him with the same strange

125
00:11:44,958 --> 00:11:50,039
interest. He actually seemed to serve as a zest to Mr. Jaggers’s wine.

126
00:11:50,039 --> 00:11:55,159
In our boyish want of discretion I dare say we took too much to drink, and I know we talked too

127
00:11:55,159 --> 00:12:00,879
much. We became particularly hot upon some boorish sneer of Drummle’s, to the effect that we were

128
00:12:00,879 --> 00:12:07,398
too free with our money. It led to my remarking, with more zeal than discretion, that it came with

129
00:12:07,398 --> 00:12:14,039
a bad grace from him, to whom Startop had lent money in my presence but a week or so before.

130
00:12:14,039 --> 00:12:17,519
“Well,” retorted Drummle; “he’ll be paid.”

131
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:20,478
“I don’t mean to imply that he won’t,” said I,

132
00:12:20,478 --> 00:12:25,159
“but it might make you hold your tongue about us and our money, I should think.”

133
00:12:25,159 --> 00:12:30,118
“You should think!” retorted Drummle. “Oh Lord!”

134
00:12:30,118 --> 00:12:34,000
“I dare say,” I went on, meaning to be very severe,

135
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,879
“that you wouldn’t lend money to any of us if we wanted it.”

136
00:12:37,879 --> 00:12:40,759
“You are right,” said Drummle. “I wouldn’t lend one of you a

137
00:12:40,759 --> 00:12:44,798
sixpence. I wouldn’t lend anybody a sixpence.”

138
00:12:44,798 --> 00:12:48,798
“Rather mean to borrow under those circumstances, I should say.”

139
00:12:48,798 --> 00:12:53,958
“You should say,” repeated Drummle. “Oh Lord!”

140
00:12:53,958 --> 00:12:58,559
This was so very aggravating—the more especially as I found myself making no

141
00:12:58,559 --> 00:13:05,159
way against his surly obtuseness—that I said, disregarding Herbert’s efforts to check me,—

142
00:13:05,159 --> 00:13:08,199
“Come, Mr. Drummle, since we are on the subject,

143
00:13:08,199 --> 00:13:13,000
I’ll tell you what passed between Herbert here and me, when you borrowed that money.”

144
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:17,359
“I don’t want to know what passed between Herbert there and you,” growled Drummle.

145
00:13:17,359 --> 00:13:24,317
And I think he added in a lower growl, that we might both go to the devil and shake ourselves.

146
00:13:24,317 --> 00:13:28,118
“I’ll tell you, however,” said I, “whether you want to know or

147
00:13:28,118 --> 00:13:33,159
not. We said that as you put it in your pocket very glad to get it,

148
00:13:33,159 --> 00:13:38,118
you seemed to be immensely amused at his being so weak as to lend it.”

149
00:13:38,118 --> 00:13:43,840
Drummle laughed outright, and sat laughing in our faces, with his hands in his pockets and his round

150
00:13:43,840 --> 00:13:51,879
shoulders raised; plainly signifying that it was quite true, and that he despised us as asses all.

151
00:13:51,879 --> 00:13:56,398
Hereupon Startop took him in hand, though with a much better grace than I had shown,

152
00:13:56,398 --> 00:14:03,080
and exhorted him to be a little more agreeable. Startop, being a lively, bright young fellow, and

153
00:14:03,080 --> 00:14:08,199
Drummle being the exact opposite, the latter was always disposed to resent him as a direct personal

154
00:14:08,199 --> 00:14:14,798
affront. He now retorted in a coarse, lumpish way, and Startop tried to turn the discussion

155
00:14:14,798 --> 00:14:20,159
aside with some small pleasantry that made us all laugh. Resenting this little success more than

156
00:14:20,159 --> 00:14:26,317
anything, Drummle, without any threat or warning, pulled his hands out of his pockets, dropped his

157
00:14:26,317 --> 00:14:32,797
round shoulders, swore, took up a large glass, and would have flung it at his adversary’s head, but

158
00:14:32,798 --> 00:14:38,719
for our entertainer’s dexterously seizing it at the instant when it was raised for that purpose.

159
00:14:38,719 --> 00:14:43,638
“Gentlemen,” said Mr. Jaggers, deliberately putting down the glass, and hauling out his gold

160
00:14:43,638 --> 00:14:50,359
repeater by its massive chain, “I am exceedingly sorry to announce that it’s half past nine.”

161
00:14:50,359 --> 00:14:55,879
On this hint we all rose to depart. Before we got to the street door, Startop was cheerily calling

162
00:14:55,879 --> 00:15:02,039
Drummle “old boy,” as if nothing had happened. But the old boy was so far from responding,

163
00:15:02,039 --> 00:15:06,840
that he would not even walk to Hammersmith on the same side of the way; so Herbert and I,

164
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:12,438
who remained in town, saw them going down the street on opposite sides; Startop leading,

165
00:15:12,438 --> 00:15:18,519
and Drummle lagging behind in the shadow of the houses, much as he was wont to follow in his boat.

166
00:15:18,520 --> 00:15:22,520
As the door was not yet shut, I thought I would leave Herbert there for a moment,

167
00:15:22,520 --> 00:15:27,599
and run upstairs again to say a word to my guardian. I found him in his dressing-room

168
00:15:27,599 --> 00:15:32,919
surrounded by his stock of boots, already hard at it, washing his hands of us.

169
00:15:32,919 --> 00:15:36,080
I told him I had come up again to say how sorry I was that anything

170
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:41,958
disagreeable should have occurred, and that I hoped he would not blame me much.

171
00:15:41,958 --> 00:15:46,879
“Pooh!” said he, sluicing his face, and speaking through the water-drops;

172
00:15:46,879 --> 00:15:50,680
“it’s nothing, Pip. I like that Spider though.”

173
00:15:50,680 --> 00:15:57,080
He had turned towards me now, and was shaking his head, and blowing, and towelling himself.

174
00:15:57,080 --> 00:16:01,039
“I am glad you like him, sir,” said I—“but I don’t.”

175
00:16:01,039 --> 00:16:06,638
“No, no,” my guardian assented; “don’t have too much to do with him. Keep as clear of him as you

176
00:16:06,638 --> 00:16:13,317
can. But I like the fellow, Pip; he is one of the true sort. Why, if I was a fortune-teller—”

177
00:16:13,317 --> 00:16:16,278
Looking out of the towel, he caught my eye.

178
00:16:16,278 --> 00:16:21,558
“But I am not a fortune-teller,” he said, letting his head drop into a festoon of towel,

179
00:16:21,559 --> 00:16:28,638
and towelling away at his two ears. “You know what I am, don’t you? Good night, Pip.”

180
00:16:28,638 --> 00:16:30,559
“Good night, sir.”

181
00:16:30,559 --> 00:16:34,879
In about a month after that, the Spider’s time with Mr. Pocket was up for good, and,

182
00:16:34,879 --> 00:16:38,638
to the great relief of all the house but Mrs. Pocket, he went home to the

183
00:16:38,638 --> 00:16:40,000
family hole. Chapter 27.
